<Base title="Why Code-First · The Counter-Argument to n8n, Make & Zapier · Pravine Mani" description="Why I refuse no-code automation rebuilds. The math: $0/mo platform vs $300/mo glue, plus version control, observability, and no vendor lock-in." jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="breadcrumb"><span class="gr">$</span> cat ~/why-code-first.md <span class="you">→ the counter-argument</span></div>
  <h1>Why code-first.<br><span class="accent">The argument against n8n, Make, and Zapier.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">I receive the same question on nearly every call: "Can you just build it in n8n?" The answer is no, and I will decline the engagement before we even discuss price. Below is the full counter-argument: in numbers, mechanics, and pattern.</p>
</section>

<section class="section manifesto">

  <div class="manifesto-body">

    <h2>The cost math.</h2>
    <p>Consider a workflow that fires 100 times per day: a daily brief, a lead-routing pipeline, an AI inbox triage. Real-world numbers I have seen at my desk this month:</p>

    <div class="compare">
      <div class="compare-col">
        <div class="ctop"><span class="lab">CODE-FIRST</span><span class="num">$73 / yr</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>Vercel Hobby: $0/mo</li>
          <li>Vercel cron + serverless: free under 100K invocations</li>
          <li>Supabase free tier: covers it</li>
          <li>Resend free tier: 3K emails/mo</li>
          <li>Anthropic API at ~$0.20/day: $73/year</li>
          <li>Domain: pre-existing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-col compare-bad">
        <div class="ctop"><span class="lab">NO-CODE STACK</span><span class="num">$720 / yr</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>n8n Cloud Starter: $20/mo</li>
          <li>Make Core: $9/mo</li>
          <li>Per-action quotas hit at ~5K events/mo</li>
          <li>Premium tier needed at scale</li>
          <li>Each new workflow = new subscription</li>
          <li>$0 of it is yours when you stop paying</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The price gap is the loud signal. <strong>The lock-in gap is the louder one.</strong></p>

    <h2>The four reasons.</h2>

    <h3>01 · Code is owned. Subscriptions are rented.</h3>
    <p>Stop paying the n8n bill and the business stops working. The workflow does not exist outside their UI. The credentials sit in their vault. The logic is hostage to their pricing changes. With code-first, you stop paying me and the workflow keeps running: it lives in your git repo, on your Vercel project, hitting your APIs. The dependency is on services you already pay for.</p>

    <h3>02 · Versioned in git. Reviewable. Revertible.</h3>
    <p>Every change to a code-first workflow is a commit. Every commit has a diff, an author, a timestamp, a CI run, and a deploy preview. A bad change can be rolled back in 60 seconds. In n8n, the change happens inside a visual editor on someone else's server: no diff, no review, no rollback unless you remembered to export the JSON before clicking save.</p>

    <h3>03 · Observable. Debuggable at 3am.</h3>
    <p>When the workflow breaks at 3am (and it will), code-first provides Vercel logs, Sentry traces, and Plausible events, all in one observability stack you already understand. n8n displays a UI screen reading "node failed" with no stack trace, no API response body, and no way to replay the event locally. I have watched founders lose four hours on a single missing API key because the no-code tool would not tell them which call failed.</p>

    <h3>04 · The integration library is a feature, not a moat.</h3>
    <p>n8n's pitch is its 500+ integrations. In practice, most engagements use three of them. The other 497 are dead weight inflating the price. For the three you need: an Anthropic SDK call is approximately 12 lines of TypeScript. A Stripe webhook handler is 60 lines. A Supabase query is one line. You are paying $20/mo for visual versions of code that fits in a single file.</p>

    <h2>What I would actually recommend.</h2>
    <p>If you have one workflow and you are not technical, <strong>use Zapier or n8n.</strong> That is the correct choice for that use case. The math does not shift until you have three or more workflows, want to ship features quickly, and the operating cost starts to matter.</p>
    <p>If you have three or more workflows, or you want to own the asset outright, <strong>hire someone to build the code-first version.</strong> That could be me. It could be a freelance developer with the same opinions. The math compounds quickly once you are past the first workflow because every additional workflow costs roughly the same: zero platform delta, approximately $0.20/day in API tokens.</p>

    <h2>The stack I default to.</h2>
    <ul class="stack-list">
      <li><strong>Code execution:</strong> Vercel serverless functions + Vercel cron</li>
      <li><strong>State:</strong> Supabase (Postgres) or Vercel KV (Redis-style)</li>
      <li><strong>AI:</strong> Anthropic API (Claude 4.7) with prompt caching from day one</li>
      <li><strong>Email:</strong> Resend (transactional) + Beehiiv (broadcast)</li>
      <li><strong>Payments:</strong> Stripe Checkout + webhook handler</li>
      <li><strong>Observability:</strong> Vercel logs + Sentry + Plausible</li>
      <li><strong>Auth:</strong> Supabase Auth (or Clerk if you want a managed surface)</li>
      <li><strong>Version control:</strong> GitHub + Vercel preview deploys</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Total platform cost for a 3-workflow business: <strong>typically $73 / year + API tokens</strong>. Compare against your current bill.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="manifesto-cta reveal">
    <h2>Have a workflow you are assembling across SaaS tools?<br><span class="accent">Let's scope the code-first version.</span></h2>
    <p>30 minutes. No pitch. You describe what you are paying for and what you want it to do; I share whether code-first is a better fit than your current stack and where the wins are. In writing, same day.</p>
    <div class="cta-row" style="justify-content: center;">
      <a href="/contact/" class="cta-btn">Book a 30-min call →</a>
      <a href="/services/" class="cta-btn secondary">See engagement shapes</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</section>

